## evaluate-operator.soar -- selection-world: 1 proposal, 1 compare, 5 applications.

## ---- operator proposal ----

## Propose evaluate-operator.
sp {selection-world*propose*selection*evaluate-operator
   :default
   (state <s> ^name selection
              ^item <item>)
  -{(state <s> ^evaluation <evaluation>)
    (<evaluation> ^super-operator <item>
                  ^value true)}
-->
   (<s> ^operator <operator> +)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator
               ^super-operator <item>)}

## ---- evaluate / compare ----

## Preference for selection select evaluate-operator indifferent.
sp {selection-world*compare*selection*select*evaluate-operator*indifferent
   :default
   (state <s> ^name selection
             -^operator-selection not-indifferent
              ^operator <operator> +)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator)
-->
   (<s> ^operator <operator> =)}

## evaluate-first / evaluate-last (v2.46, World-Conventions L15-026): disabled in v2.47 (JL, 23 Nov 2026).
## v2.46 turned a task's ^evaluate-first / ^evaluate-last marks on proposed operators into best and worst preferences on
## the evaluate-operator choices. Two problems: (1) best also ranked a marked evaluate-operator above the selection's
## own best operators (init-current-evaluation-depth, init-remaining-operators, decrement-remaining-operators), so a
## nested look-ahead could start before its depth limit was set and run with none; (2) with add-osk on, the preference
## and the task conditions behind the mark enter the rules learned from the look-ahead (Tower of London test: 178 of 212
## learned rules tested a mark; learning about 4 times slower per decision, no solution in 580 s). The marks are now
## ignored; a task that still sets them gets no preference from them.

## ---- operator application ----

## Apply selection -> sets evaluation, super-operator.
sp {selection-world*apply*selection*state*evaluation
   :default
   (state <s> ^name selection
              ^operator <operator>)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator
               ^super-operator <super-operator>)
  -{(<s> ^evaluation <evaluation2>)
    (<evaluation2> ^super-operator <super-operator>)}
-->
   (<s> ^evaluation <evaluation>)
   (<evaluation> ^super-operator <super-operator>)}

## Apply selection (o-support) -> sets evaluation.
sp {selection-world*apply*selection*evaluate-operator*evaluation
   :default
   :o-support
   (state <s> ^name selection
              ^operator <operator>
              ^evaluation <evaluation>)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator
              -^evaluation <evaluation>
               ^super-operator <super-operator>)
   (<evaluation> ^super-operator <super-operator>)
-->
   (<operator> ^evaluation <evaluation>)}

## Apply selection -> sets super-problem-space.
sp {selection-world*apply*selection*evaluate-operator*super-problem-space
   :default
   (state <s> ^name selection
              ^operator <operator>
              ^superstate.problem-space <super-problem-space>)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator)
-->
   (<operator> ^super-problem-space <super-problem-space>)}

## Apply selection -> sets superstate.
sp {selection-world*apply*selection*evaluate-operator*superstate
   :default
   (state <s> ^name selection
              ^operator <operator>
              ^superstate <superstate>)
   (<operator> ^name evaluate-operator)
-->
   (<operator> ^superstate <superstate>)}

## Apply evaluate-operator.
sp {selection-world*apply*evaluate-operator*print
   :default
   (state <s> ^operator <op>)
   (<op> ^name evaluate-operator
         ^super-operator <super-operator>)
   (<super-operator> ^name <name>)
-->
   (log 1 |Evaluate operator: | <name>)
}
